INT. STAGECOACH - MOVING - DAY

BOONE MCCREADY, 40s, a bounty hunter, sits wedged against the window with a battered rifle across his lap. JOSIAH PRICE, 60s, a whiskey drummer in a checked suit, fans himself with a hat. LILA STONE, 20s, in a grey travelling dress, holds a hatbox on her knees.

BOONE
How far to Prosper Creek?

JOSIAH
Another six hours. If the axle holds.

LILA
It won't.

JOSIAH
Young lady, you can't know that.

LILA
I heard it crack at the last stop.

BOONE
She's right.

JOSIAH
You heard it too?

BOONE
I heard the driver pray.

JOSIAH
Drivers always pray.

BOONE
Not that loud.

LILA
What happens if it breaks?

BOONE
We walk.

JOSIAH
In this heat?

BOONE
In any heat.

LILA
I can walk.

BOONE
Can you walk in those shoes?

LILA
I walked here in them.

JOSIAH
From where?

LILA
From Ohio.

JOSIAH
Ohio's two thousand miles.

LILA
Then I've had practice.

BOONE
(a dry chuckle)
She'll do.

JOSIAH
What's in the hatbox, miss?

LILA
A hat.

JOSIAH
Just a hat?

LILA
Mostly.

BOONE
Don't ask, Josiah.

JOSIAH
I'm only curious.

BOONE
Curious gets you buried at Prosper Creek.

JOSIAH
Is that so.

BOONE
I've dug the graves.

LILA
You dig graves?

BOONE
I dig what pays.

LILA
And what pays today?

BOONE
Ask your friend.

JOSIAH
I beg your pardon?

BOONE
Mr. Price. Open your coat.

JOSIAH
I will do no such thing.

BOONE
Open it.

JOSIAH
This is outrageous.

LILA
(quietly)
He has a gun, Mr. McCready.

BOONE
I know. I saw it at the depot.

JOSIAH
Madam, you are mistaken.

LILA
Sir, I'm not.

JOSIAH
(after a long breath)
Well. That changes the arithmetic.

BOONE
It does.

JOSIAH
Who is the bounty on?

BOONE
Not you, Josiah.

JOSIAH
Then who?

BOONE
Her.

Lila's hands tighten on the hatbox. The coach lurches. Something cracks beneath them. Far off, the driver begins to pray very, very loudly.
